Initially, with the switch to the right, capacitor \(C_1\) is directly connected across the \(9\,\text{V}\) battery.

So the initial charge on \(C_1\) is

\[
Q_0 = C_1 V = 1\,\mu\text{F} \times 9\,\text{V} = 9\,\mu\text{C}
\]

When the switch is thrown to the left, \(C_1\) is connected in parallel with the series combination of \(C_2\) and \(C_3\). The battery is disconnected, so charge redistributes.

The equivalent capacitance of \(C_2\) and \(C_3\) in series is

\[
C_{23} = \frac{C_2 C_3}{C_2+C_3}
= \frac{1 \times 1}{1+1}
= 0.5\,\mu\text{F}
\]

Now \(C_1\) and \(C_{23}\) are in parallel, so total equivalent capacitance is

\[
C_{\text{eq}} = C_1 + C_{23}
= 1 + 0.5
= 1.5\,\mu\text{F}
\]

The final common voltage across them is

\[
V_f = \frac{Q_0}{C_{\text{eq}}}
= \frac{9\,\mu\text{C}}{1.5\,\mu\text{F}}
= 6\,\text{V}
\]

The charge on the series branch \(C_2,C_3\) is therefore

\[
Q = C_{23} V_f
= 0.5\,\mu\text{F} \times 6\,\text{V}
= 3\,\mu\text{C}
\]

Since \(C_2\) and \(C_3\) are in series, they carry the same charge.  
Thus the final charge on \(C_2\) is

\[
\boxed{3\,\mu\text{C}}
\]

**Correct option: 3. \(3\,\mu\text{C}\)**

The \(5\,\Omega\) and \(10\,\Omega\) resistors are in parallel, so the same voltage \(V\) is applied across both.

Heat dissipated per second = electrical power:

\[
P=\frac{V^2}{R}
\]

So,

\[
\frac{P_{5\Omega}}{P_{10\Omega}}
=
\frac{V^2/5}{V^2/10}
=
\frac{10}{5}
=
\frac{2}{1}
\]

\[
\boxed{2:1}
\]

Correct option: **(2) \(2:1\)**
